{
  "cards": [
    {
      "question": "When you flip a light switch, the electrons in the wire mostly… ",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Race from the switch to the bulb",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Jiggle in place, passing energy along",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Leave the wire and fly through the air",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Electrons in a wire barely move from their spots. Each one bumps the next, passing energy along, so the bulb lights almost instantly even though the electrons drift very slowly."
    },
    {
      "question": "Why does a sunburn-causing beam of sunlight carry more punch than a dim lamp of the same brightness?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "It is hotter to the touch",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Each of its light packets carries more energy",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "It travels faster through the air",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Light arrives in packets called photons. Ultraviolet photons carry more energy each than visible ones, so a dim UV source can do damage that a bright ordinary lamp cannot."
    },
    {
      "question": "A single grain of sand is so small that a quantum particle inside it is…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Able to be in two places at once",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Always stuck in one fixed spot",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Invisible because it has no mass",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Large objects like sand grains behave classically in practice. Their quantum weirdness averages out, so the wave-like spreading of their particles is far too small to notice."
    },
    {
      "question": "When you look at a tiny electron, it seems to be… ",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "A small hard ball of matter",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A cloud of probabilities until measured",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "A perfectly sharp dot in one place",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Physicists describe an electron with a wave function that gives the odds of finding it at each point. A measurement gives one definite result, and the wave pattern shapes which results are likely."
    },
    {
      "question": "Which is more energetic, a photon of red light or a photon of blue light?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Red light",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Blue light",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Blue light, because photon energy rises with frequency. Blue light has a higher frequency than red, so each blue photon carries more energy than each red one."
    },
    {
      "question": "A quantum computer's basic unit of information is called a…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Quantum bit, or qubit",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Super byte",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Photon pixel",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "A qubit. Unlike a regular bit that is 0 or 1, a qubit can exist in a blend of both states until it is measured, which is part of why these machines work differently."
    },
    {
      "question": "Heavier atoms like gold absorb and emit light in a way that is…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Identical to every other element",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Distinct, like a unique fingerprint",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Only visible with X-rays",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Each element's electrons occupy only certain energy levels. Jumps between those levels produce light at specific colors, which is why neon signs glow in characteristic hues and spectra identify elements."
    },
    {
      "question": "In a double-slit experiment, when no one checks which slit a particle passes through, the result looks like…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Two separate bands, one per slit",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A wave pattern of interference bands",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "A single blob behind the middle",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "An interference pattern of many bands, the same kind water waves make. Particles build up that pattern one hit at a time, which shows that each particle behaves as if it went through both slits."
