{
  "cards": [
    {
      "question": "On a hot July afternoon, the pendulum clock hanging in your hallway probably runs…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Faster",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Slower",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Exactly the same",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Slower. Heat makes the brass rod grow a little longer, and a longer pendulum swings more slowly, so the clock falls behind."
    },
    {
      "question": "A clock with no hanging weights gets its power from…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "A coiled spring",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "A battery in the base",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Air pressure in the case",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "A coiled spring, called the mainspring. As it slowly unwinds, it turns the gears, and winding the clock stores that energy again."
    },
    {
      "question": "Clock oil left inside an antique movement for decades usually ends up…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Thinner and cleaner",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Sticky and gummy",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Exactly as it was",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Sticky and gummy. Old oil slowly thickens into a varnish-like residue, which is why restorers take a clock apart and clean it before adding fresh oil."
    },
    {
      "question": "The steady tick and tock comes from a small part that lets the gears move in tiny, timed steps. It is called the…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Escapement",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Mainspring",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Hairspring",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Escapement. Each swing of the pendulum releases one tooth of the gear train, so the wheels advance in small, even steps and make the tick and tock."
    },
    {
      "question": "Which takes about the same time as a wide swing of a pendulum, not a short one?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "A short swing",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A wide swing",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Neither, a swing takes about the same time",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "A swing takes about the same time either way. For small swings, the time of one full swing depends mostly on the pendulum's length, not on how far it travels."
    },
    {
      "question": "When oiling an antique clock, a beginner's most common mistake is…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Using too little oil",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Using too much oil",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Oiling only the case",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Using too much oil. Extra oil creeps onto parts it should not reach and collects dust, so restorers add just a tiny drop to each pivot."
    }
  ]
}
